WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.840
ED GLAESER: We're here in Central Park, which is one of the world's great urban spaces.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:09.110
Open squares had existed in cities for a millenia, but they were primarily

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:13.500
places of commerce, not sylvan refuges from the strife of city life.

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:14.190
After all,

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:19.060
nature was seen as something dangerous, looming menacingly beyond city walls.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:22.120
It was only in the 19th century, when romantic poets like Keats and

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:25.010
Wordsworth began to panegyrize nature.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:29.240
Cities filled up with migrants from farms who presumably missed a little bit of

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.080
grass and a few flowers.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.040
The downsides to extreme density, the fetid water,

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:37.050
the unclean air, the mountains of human refuge,

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:41.800
made the idea of a sylvan retreat even more appealing by the mid-19th century.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:47.810
New York City's 1811 grid plan gave little space for open parks, only the parade.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:51.900
Now Madison Square Park was marked on a plan as being a clearly visible

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:52.960
urban park.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:57.730
William Cullen Bryant, the editor of the New York Evening Post, took up the call for

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:00.020
a larger park here in New York.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:04.450
Bryant had been born in a log cabin in Massachusetts, but he came to New York and

00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:08.250
enjoyed huge success as the editor of the New York Evening Post.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.850
He was joined by Andrew Jackson Downing,

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:14.210
the founding father of landscape architecture in America and

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:19.520
together, they waged a campaign for a greener, cleaner New York.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.660
Their campaign brings forth legislation in 1853 which sets aside

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:26.070
700 acres for a park.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.710
It also establishes a park commission.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:32.600
So much of the great 19th century infrastructure in New York

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.950
was built by state-appointed commissions, not local government.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:37.700
The Croton Aqueduct, a state commission.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:39.370
The Erie Canal, a state commission.

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:41.728
Central Park, a state commission.

00:01:41.728 --> 00:01:45.610
The park commission held a contest to come up with the best plan for

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:49.120
a park that would enrich the lives of ordinary New Yorkers.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:53.830
It was won by two young men, Calvert Vaux and Frederick Law Olmsted.

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:57.140
Vaux had been a partner in Downing's office.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.730
Olmsted had no experience in landscape architecture.

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:02.680
He'd been a journalist, whose writings on the antebellum south

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:06.470
still contained some of the most vivid descriptions of that slave economy.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:09.420
Together, they designed a green swathed masterpiece,

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:12.440
a people's park that would be open to all.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:17.650
They eschewed vast marble arches that would bar the entrance for the poor.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:20.978
Instead, they have simple, easy entryways that welcome in anyone.

00:02:20.978 --> 00:02:24.690
And they filled their park with marvels like this pond,

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:28.980
which in the summer is filled with boats that bob up and down.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:33.490
There are 36 bridges designed by Calvert Vaux himself throughout the park.

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:37.170
There's a sheep's meadow where sheep themselves gamboled on

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:38.110
the green until 1934.

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:42.680
There's a castle, the Belvedere that rises out of the mists.

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:45.760
It's not even short on the attempts to impress civic values.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:49.900
It's filled with statues of 19th century icons like Sir Walter Scott and

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:51.520
William Shakespeare.

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.270
Of course, the park does create challenges, most importantly,

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:57.000
maintenance and programming.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:59.320
All of this green space and

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:04.180
all of this humanity can create a mess, the park can easily get run down.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:07.650
So over the course of the late 19th and early 20th centuries,

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:11.690
when Tammany Hall had little use for a park that abutted the buildings that were

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:15.760
occupied by their wealthy political enemies, the park decayed.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:20.380
It was only in 1933, when the reforming mayor Fiorello La Guardia was elected

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:22.230
that the park acquired a new life.

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:28.150
He appointed the redoubtable Robert Moses who took the park firmly in hand.

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:31.900
Moses is responsible for much of the park, as indeed he is responsible for much of

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:37.300
New York City's infrastructure, but he's particularly associated with the zoo.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:43.240
Central Park had had animals since 1859 when private donors started a menagerie.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:47.210
But it's really Moses who gave the zoo its modern form,

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:50.790
a form that has delighted New York's children for generations.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:54.870
I particularly treasured the Delacorte Clock Tower that was only built in 1965,

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:58.880
where the animals dance around to the sound of the chimes.

00:03:58.880 --> 00:03:59.690
The park, again,

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:03.900
suffered a decay in 60s and 70s when New York's crime got out of control.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:06.270
As a kid, I always felt that this was a place of danger,

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:09.700
at least apart from the prime visiting hours on weekends.

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:13.040
When I had to commute through the park during a transit strike, I talked loudly

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:16.680
to myself, in the hopes that apparent insanity would deter would be muggers.

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:20.120
The famed jogger case is a particularly notorious example

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:21.860
of crime in Central Park.

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:25.120
Of course, the park did become safe again as the city became safe, and

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.190
now thousands of joggers go through the park daily enjoying its pleasures.

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:32.680
Programming is the other challenge, this great urban space calls for

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:34.700
humanity to use it well.

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:38.880
Perhaps the most important entrepreneur in that regard was Joseph Papp who in

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.600
the 1960s began his Shakespeare in the Park, which brought some of the world's

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:46.920
greatest actors, right here to the park, to performances that were free.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:49.758
I have fond memories of watching Kevin Kline in Henry the 5th there,

00:04:49.758 --> 00:04:51.310
40 years ago.

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:56.040
This park is an urban marvel, and it is a tribute, both to the vision of Vaux and

00:04:56.040 --> 00:05:00.600
Olmsted and to the value of having green space within the city.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:04.270
It is also a tribute to the generosity of New York's philanthropists who have

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:07.120
help to improve and maintain the park over the years.

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:10.310
There are legitimate public policy questions about Central Park.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:11.720
Is it too large?

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:14.440
Would it be better if it were carved into more smaller parks and

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:15.909
spread more evenly throughout the city?

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:18.790
But while you can ask those questions,

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:22.420
there's no doubt that this park is an absolute treasure for New York, and

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:25.000
a model of what truly beautiful urban space can be.

